.\" Manual page for rasterminal.
.\" Kept in sync by hand with src/args.cpp's --help text; update both together.
.\" The .TH version and date track src/version.h; bump them when cutting a release.
.TH RASTERMINAL 1 "2026-06-27" "rasterminal 0.1.0-alpha.1" "User Commands"
.\" ASCII fallbacks for the accented author name: groff's -Tascii table drops
.\" u00FD (y-acute) entirely, so without these the name renders "Ulicn" (a
.\" letter short). utf8/latin1 still use the real glyph where the device has it.
.\" mandoc ignores .fchar (groff extension) and substitutes its own ASCII
.\" transliteration, so the two "unsupported request" lint notices are expected.
.fchar \[u010D] c
.fchar \[u00FD] y
.SH NAME
rasterminal \- render a 3D model in the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rasterminal
[\fIoptions\fR]
.I model
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rasterminal
is a cross-platform 3D model viewer that renders entirely in the terminal:
as real pixels on terminals with kitty or sixel graphics support, detected at
startup (see
.BR \-\-graphics ),
and as unicode half-block characters with ANSI color escapes elsewhere, in
24-bit (truecolor) where the terminal supports it with an automatic 256-color
fallback (see
.B ENVIRONMENT
and
.BR \-\-color ).
.PP
The
.I model
operand is required. A lone
.B \-
is treated as a positional operand, not stdin.
.PP
Interactive rendering requires that both standard input and standard output be
terminals; if either is piped or redirected,
.B rasterminal
exits with an error rather than writing escape sequences into the stream. The
headless
.B \-\-bench
mode is exempt and runs with redirected streams.
.SH SUPPORTED FORMATS
.TP
.B .obj
Wavefront OBJ with optional .mtl (diffuse/specular/normal maps).
.TP
.B .ply
ASCII or binary (little- and big-endian).
.TP
.B .stl
ASCII or binary. The format's conventional Z\-up orientation is remapped so models load upright.
ASCII files are rejected if any single line exceeds 64 KB (a malformed\-input guard; the format puts one short statement per line).
.TP
.B .gltf, .glb
glTF 2.0 (PBR materials, textures, node transforms).
.TP
.B Other formats through Assimp
AMF, 3DS, AC, ASE, Assbin, B3D, BVH, Collada, DXF, CSM, HMP, IrrMesh,
IQM, IRR, LWO/LWS, MD2/MD3/MD5/MDC/MDL, NFF/NDO/OFF, Ogre, OpenGEX,
MS3D, COB, Blender, IFC, XGL, FBX, Q3D/Q3BSP, RAW, SIB, SMD, Terragen,
Unreal 3D, DirectX X, X3D, 3MF and MMD.
The fallback imports static geometry and common materials and textures.
It flattens scene transforms and does not retain animation, skinning, cameras,
lights or format-specific metadata. Native extensions are never retried through
Assimp.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Initial state
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-shading " " \fImode\fR
Initial shading mode:
.BR wireframe | flat | phong .
Default:
.BR phong .
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bg " " \fIcolor\fR
Initial background color:
.BR black | gray | white
.RB ( grey
also accepted). Default:
.BR black .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-lighting " " \fImode\fR
Initial lighting mode:
.BR dual | single | flat .
Default:
.BR dual .
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-wireframe\-color " " \fIcolor\fR
Initial wireframe edge color:
.BR white | red | green | yellow | cyan | magenta .
Default:
.BR white .
.TP
.BI \-\-yaw " DEG"
Initial camera yaw in degrees, in
.BR [\-180,\ 180] ;
positive turns the model left on screen (the same direction as
.B \-\-spin\-direction
.BR left ).
Also sets the
.B \-\-bench
camera. Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BI \-\-pitch " DEG"
Initial camera pitch in degrees, in
.BR [\-180,\ 180] ;
negative looks down from above,
.B \-90
is top\-down, and values past \(+-90 view the model upside down. Under
.B \-\-first\-person
the value is clamped to just inside straight up and straight down, the same limit
the mode holds while looking, since a flying camera must not start upside down or
in a pose it cannot itself hold. Also sets the
.B \-\-bench
camera. Default:
.BR \-17.2 .
.TP
.BI \-\-zoom " FACTOR"
Initial zoom as an apparent\-size multiplier of the auto\-fit framing, in
.BR [0.2,\ 100] :
.B 2
starts twice as close,
.B 0.2
fully zoomed out. The bounds equal the range the scroll wheel reaches in orbit
mode; under
.B \-\-first\-person
the wheel sets movement speed instead, and this sets how far back the camera
starts. Also sets the
.B \-\-bench
camera. Default:
.BR 1 .
.TP
.BI \-\-first\-person\-speed " FACTOR"
Initial movement speed as a multiplier of the model\-scaled default, in
.BR [0.05,\ 20] ,
the same range
.BR +/\- " and the wheel"
move within. Requires
.BR \-\-first\-person ,
without which the value could never come into play. Default:
.BR 1 .
.TP
.BR \-\-cull ", " \-\-no\-cull
Enable or disable backface culling initially; the later flag wins. Default:
.BR \-\-cull .
.TP
.BR \-\-texture ", " \-\-no\-texture
Enable or disable texture rendering initially; the later flag wins. Default:
.BR \-\-texture .
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-spin ", " \-\-no\-spin
Enable or disable auto-rotation initially; the later flag wins. Default:
.BR \-\-no\-spin .
.SS Runtime and load
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-threads " " [\fIN\fR]
Worker threads. Bare
.B \-j
uses all cores;
.BI \-j " N"
uses
.IR N ,
clamped to the CPU thread count. The default depends on the backend: all cores
under
.BR kitty " and " sixel ,
and
.B min(cores,4)
under
.BR blocks ,
whose frames are roughly a hundredth the size and already finish inside the
frame cap. Loading a model always uses all cores, whatever the backend.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-fps " " [\fIN\fR]
Frame cap. Bare
.B \-f
is uncapped;
.BI \-f " N"
caps at
.I N
fps. Default:
.BR 30 .
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-bench " " [\fIN\fR]
Headless benchmark: run
.I N
frames (default
.BR 200 )
with warmup discarded, print a structured timing/fps/throughput report to
standard error, then exit. No terminal I/O.
.TP
.BI \-\-bench\-size " WxH"
Bench framebuffer size in pixels (default
.BR 200x120 ).
Requires
.BR \-\-bench .
.TP
.BI \-\-bench\-warmup " N"
Bench warmup frames discarded (default
.BR 20 ;
.B 0
for none). Requires
.BR \-\-bench .
.TP
.BI \-\-smooth\-angle " DEG"
Crease angle in degrees, in
.BR [0,\ 180] ,
for normals computed when a file has no normals: below it adjacent faces smooth,
above it they hard-edge. Default
.BR 60 ;
.B 0
is faceted,
.B 180
is fully smooth. Ignored when an OBJ authors smoothing groups.
Assimp-backed formats cap this setting at Assimp's 175-degree maximum.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " mode"
Color output:
.B truecolor
(alias
.BR 24bit )
forces 24-bit output,
.B 256
forces 256-color output, and
.B auto
(the default) detects the terminal's capability from the environment (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ,
including the GNU screen 256-color floor that
.B truecolor
overrides).
Forcing a color mode does not bypass the
.B TERM=dumb
rejection or the Windows VT-capability gate.
With the kitty graphics backend the image is transmitted as 24-bit pixels and
with sixel as the fixed 240-color palette regardless; this option then affects
only the HUD line.
.TP
.BI \-\-graphics " mode"
Rendering backend.
.B kitty
draws real pixels through the kitty graphics protocol,
.B sixel
draws real pixels through sixel graphics,
.B blocks
draws unicode half-block cells, and
.B auto
(the default) prefers kitty when the terminal answers the startup capability
query, then sixel when its device attributes reply advertises it, and blocks
otherwise.
.IP
A sixel image is always the 240-color xterm palette at native resolution. Its
height is kept to whole 6-pixel sixel bands, and it never paints the last
terminal row, even under
.BR \-\-no\-hud ,
since an image touching that row scrolls the screen every frame. It is capped
to the maximum image size the terminal advertises, when it advertises one, and
centered when a limit leaves it smaller than the window and the cell size is
known rather than guessed. xterm defaults to a 1000x1000 pixel maximum and
discards, rather than clips, anything larger. Its
.B maxStringParse
resource (default 600000) silently drops any frame whose sixel encoding is
larger, which very busy scenes can exceed; raise it with
.BR "xterm \-xrm '*maxStringParse: 10000000'" .
.IP
Forcing
.B kitty
or
.B sixel
still requires detection to succeed, since a terminal without the protocol
would silently show nothing. Both are an error under tmux and GNU screen:
kitty is not passed through there, and sixel support is build-dependent and
not detected.
.TP
.BI \-\-spin\-speed " DEG/S"
Auto-rotation speed in degrees per second, used whenever spinning is active
(via
.B \-\-spin
or the Space toggle). Any positive number; zero and negative values are
rejected (direction is set by
.BR \-\-spin\-direction ).
Default
.BR 45 .
.TP
.BI \-\-spin\-direction " dir"
Auto-rotation direction:
.B left
or
.BR right ,
the way the model's front face moves on screen. Default
.BR left .
.SS Session-fixed toggles
.TP
.BR \-\-ao ", " \-\-no\-ao
Enable or disable baked ambient occlusion; the later flag wins. Default:
.BR \-\-ao .
.TP
.BR \-\-hud ", " \-\-no\-hud
Show or hide the HUD status line; the later flag wins. Default:
.BR \-\-hud .
.TP
.BR \-\-input ", " \-\-no\-input
Enable or disable the keyboard and mouse controls; the later flag wins. Default:
.BR \-\-input .
Under
.B \-\-no\-input
every binding in
.B CONTROLS
is ignored except
.BR Q ,
which still quits, as does Ctrl+C. Input is still read (so it cannot pile up in
the terminal) and the mouse stays claimed by the viewer, so a drag neither orbits
nor selects text. Auto-rotation, terminal resizing, and
.B \-\-bench
are unaffected.
.TP
.BR \-\-first\-person ", " \-\-no\-first\-person
Use a free\-flying first\-person camera instead of the turntable; the later flag
wins. Default:
.BR \-\-no\-first\-person .
There is no gravity, collision or ground plane: the camera flies, and passes
through geometry. WASD moves, the arrows and a mouse drag look,
.BR E " and " V
rise and fall along world up, and
.BR +/\- " and the wheel"
set the movement speed, which is scaled to the model so it feels the same at any
size. Pitch is clamped to straight up and straight down so the horizon never
inverts, and the camera cannot fly further from the model than the orbit zoom can
already reach. Auto\-rotation still works and becomes a panorama from wherever the
camera stands. The launch pose is still the one
.BR \-\-yaw ", " \-\-pitch " and " \-\-zoom
select, with
.B \-\-pitch
clamped as described above.
.B \-\-bench
ignores this flag entirely, so a benchmark measures the same camera either way.
.PP
Note that
.B \-\-spin\-direction
is defined by the way the model's front face moves on screen. Under
.B \-\-first\-person
nothing is being orbited, so the setting names the way the view itself turns;
.B left
still turns left.
.SS Meta
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and license information and exit.
.SH OPTION SYNTAX
A long flag that takes a value accepts it with a space or
.RB ' = '
.RB ( \-\-shading
.BR flat ", " \-\-shading=flat ).
Short flags take a value with a space or in compact form
.RB ( \-s
.BR flat ", " \-sflat ).
String values are case-insensitive.
.PP
Boolean flags take no value:
.B \-\-cull=on
is an error, not a synonym for
.BR \-\-cull .
The paired flags listed above (for example
.B \-\-cull
and
.BR \-\-no\-cull )
select the two states directly, and where both appear the later one on the
command line wins.
.PP
No-argument short options may be clustered behind one
.RB ' \- '
with at most one value-taking option last
.RB ( \-Sj4
is
.BR \-S " " \-j4 ).
A bare
.B \-\-
terminates option parsing: every following token is the model operand, even one
beginning with
.RB ' \- '.
.SH CONTROLS
While rendering:
.TP
.B 1\-3
Select shading mode.
.TP
.B Space
Toggle spin.
.TP
.B WASD, arrows, mouse drag
Orbit the camera.
.TP
.B +/\-, scroll
Zoom.
.TP
.B B
Cycle background.
.TP
.B L
Cycle lighting.
.TP
.B R
Reset the camera and every runtime setting to the state selected by the command-line flags (their defaults when not passed).
.TP
.B C
Cycle wireframe color.
.TP
.B K
Toggle backface culling.
.TP
.B T
Toggle textures.
.TP
.BR Q ", " Ctrl+C
Quit.
.PP
Under
.B \-\-first\-person
the camera bindings change: WASD moves instead of orbiting, the arrows and a
mouse drag look,
.BR E " and " V
move up and down, and
.BR +/\- " and the wheel"
set the movement speed instead of zooming. Everything else in the list is
unchanged.
.PP
Under
.B \-\-no\-input
none of the above act except
.BR Q " and " Ctrl+C .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including
.BR \-\-help " and " \-\-version ).
.TP
.B 1
Usage error (unknown flag, missing or invalid value, invalid flag
combination, unexpected argument),
model load failure, or a terminal
.B rasterminal
cannot draw to (outside
.BR \-\-bench ):
standard input or output is not a terminal,
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb ,
a Windows console cannot enable virtual-terminal escape processing, or a
forced
.B \-\-graphics kitty
or
.B \-\-graphics sixel
backend is not detected (see
.BR \-\-graphics ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B rasterminal
reads these variables to pick the color mode when rendering interactively, and,
as noted below, to gate the graphics backends under a multiplexer. The headless
.B \-\-bench
mode reads none of them.
.PP
Detection runs in a fixed order and the first match wins. It applies only under
.B \-\-color auto
(the default), since any other
.B \-\-color
value forces the choice, but
.B TERM=dumb
is fatal either way, even when
.B COLORTERM
claims truecolor. Real GNU screen (a non-empty
.B STY
variable, or a screen-family
.B TERM
without a non-empty
.B TMUX
variable) is floored to 256-color next, before
.B COLORTERM
is consulted, since the inherited
.B COLORTERM
describes the outer terminal and screen 4.x renders 24-bit color as garbage.
tmux is exempt: it sets
.B TMUX
and resolves 24-bit output against the outer terminal itself; under screen 5
configured for truecolor, force
.BR "\-\-color truecolor" .
A
.B COLORTERM
truecolor signal in turn wins over a
.B TERM
that would otherwise map to 256-color.
.TP
.B COLORTERM
.B truecolor
or
.B 24bit
selects 24-bit output, except inside real GNU screen (above).
.TP
.B TERM
.B dumb
makes
.B rasterminal
exit with an error (a dumb terminal cannot render escape sequences at all); this
is checked before
.BR COLORTERM .
A screen-family name without tmux takes the GNU screen 256-color floor (above).
A truecolor hint (the
.B \-direct
terminfo family, or a name containing
.B truecolor
or
.BR 24bit )
or a known truecolor terminal name (kitty, WezTerm, Alacritty, Ghostty, foot,
Contour; this keeps ssh sessions, which do not forward
.BR COLORTERM ,
on 24-bit) selects 24-bit output. Any other non-empty value gets 256-color
output. When
.B TERM
is unset or empty and
.B COLORTERM
carries no truecolor signal, the default is 256-color (24-bit on native
Windows consoles).
.TP
.B TMUX
A non-empty value marks a tmux session and exempts a screen-family
.B TERM
from the GNU screen floor, unless
.B STY
marks a screen session, which always wins. Separately, the graphics
capability query is skipped when
.B TMUX
is set (even empty) or
.B TERM
is screen- or tmux-prefixed, so under a multiplexer
.B \-\-graphics auto
falls back to half-blocks and forcing a pixel backend is an error,
regardless of color mode.
.TP
.B STY
A non-empty value is GNU screen's own session marker and applies the
256-color floor whatever
.B TERM
and
.B TMUX
say.
.PP
.B \-\-color
overrides the detected mode.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Render a model with default settings:
.B rasterminal model.glb
.TP
Start in wireframe with a white background, spinning:
.B rasterminal \-s wireframe \-b white \-S model.obj
.TP
Benchmark 500 frames headlessly at a fixed size:
.B rasterminal \-\-bench 500 \-\-bench\-size 400x240 model.stl
